ATF SQUARE — ORDER HISTORY EXPLANATION
============================================

This document explains how Order History works in the ATF Square Home.jsx project.

1. WHAT IS ORDER HISTORY?
-------------------------
Order History stores the orders that the customer has placed from the website.

In the current project, Order History is stored in the browser using localStorage.

Important:
- It is NOT stored in a database.
- It is NOT connected to a user account.
- It is specific to the browser/device.
- Clearing browser localStorage can remove the saved history.


2. ORDER HISTORY STATE
----------------------
Inside Home(), we create two states:

const [orderHistory, setOrderHistory] = useState([]);
const [orderHistoryOpen, setOrderHistoryOpen] = useState(false);

orderHistory:
- Stores all previous orders.
- It is an array.

orderHistoryOpen:
- Controls whether the Order History popup is visible.
- false = popup closed
- true = popup open

 

3. WHY IS orderHistory AN ARRAY?
--------------------------------
There can be many orders.

Example:

[
  order1,
  order2,
  order3
]

Each object represents one complete order.


4. LOADING ORDER HISTORY
------------------------
When the page loads, we check localStorage:

useEffect(() => {
  try {
    const savedOrders = localStorage.getItem("atf_order_history");

    if (savedOrders) {
      setOrderHistory(JSON.parse(savedOrders));
    }
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("ORDER HISTORY LOAD ERROR:", error);
  }
}, []);

What happens:

Step 1:
localStorage stores data as a string.

Step 2:
getItem("atf_order_history") gets that string.

Step 3:
JSON.parse() converts the string back into JavaScript data.

Step 4:
setOrderHistory() puts that data into React state.

Flow:

localStorage
     ↓
getItem()
     ↓
JSON.parse()
     ↓
setOrderHistory()
     ↓
orderHistory state


5. SAVING ORDER HISTORY
-----------------------
Whenever orderHistory changes, another useEffect saves it:

useEffect(() => {
  try {
    localStorage.setItem(
      "atf_order_history",
      JSON.stringify(orderHistory)
    );
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("ORDER HISTORY SAVE ERROR:", error);
  }
}, [orderHistory]);

The important part is:

JSON.stringify(orderHistory)

React state is JavaScript data, but localStorage stores strings.

Therefore:

JavaScript array/object
        ↓
JSON.stringify()
        ↓
string
        ↓
localStorage


6. WHY [orderHistory] IS IN useEffect
-------------------------------------
The dependency array:

[orderHistory]

means:

"Run this effect whenever orderHistory changes."

For example:

Initial:
orderHistory = []

After first order:
orderHistory = [order1]

After second order:
orderHistory = [order2, order1]

Every change causes the updated history to be saved.


7. CREATING A NEW ORDER
-----------------------
When the customer clicks "Order on WhatsApp", the code creates a newOrder object.

Example structure:

const newOrder = {
  orderId: `ATF-${Date.now()}`,

  date: new Date().toISOString(),

  orderType,

  customer: {
    name: orderForm.name,
    mobile: orderForm.mobile,
    notes: orderForm.notes,
  },

  address: orderType === "delivery" ? addressText : "",

  items: cart.map((item) => ({
    cartItemId: item.cartItemId,
    productId: item.product.product_id,
    productName: item.product.product_name,
    quantity: item.quantity,
    price: item.price,
    preference: item.preference || "",
    variation: item.variation
      ? item.variation.variation_name
      : "",
    addons: item.addons?.map((addon) => ({
      id: addon.addon_id,
      name: addon.multiple_addon_name,
      price: addon.multiple_addon_price,
    })) || [],
  })),

  total,

  status: "Placed",
};


8. WHAT DOES newOrder CONTAIN?
------------------------------
orderId:
Unique order identifier.

Example:
ATF-1760000000000

date:
The date/time when the order was created.

orderType:
Either:

"pickup"

or:

"delivery"

customer:
Contains:

- name
- mobile
- notes

address:
Contains the delivery address when the order is a delivery order.

items:
Contains all products ordered.

total:
Final order amount.

status:
Current status of the order.

Example:

"Placed"


9. ADDING THE NEW ORDER TO HISTORY
----------------------------------
Creating newOrder is NOT enough.

We must also add it to orderHistory:

setOrderHistory((currentOrders) => [
  newOrder,
  ...currentOrders,
]);

This means:

currentOrders
    ↓
newOrder + old orders
    ↓
updated orderHistory


10. WHY newOrder IS PUT FIRST
-----------------------------
The code uses:

[
  newOrder,
  ...currentOrders
]

So the newest order appears first.

Example:

Before:

[
  Order 2,
  Order 1
]

New order:

Order 3

After:

[
  Order 3,
  Order 2,
  Order 1
]


11. WHAT DOES ...currentOrders MEAN?
------------------------------------
The spread operator copies all existing orders.

Example:

const currentOrders = [
  order2,
  order1
];

Then:

[
  newOrder,
  ...currentOrders
]

becomes:

[
  newOrder,
  order2,
  order1
]


12. OPENING ORDER HISTORY
-------------------------
The Orders button uses:

<button
  type="button"
  className="order-history-button"
  onClick={() => setOrderHistoryOpen(true)}
>
  Orders
</button>

When clicked:

orderHistoryOpen
false
   ↓
true

React then displays the Order History popup.


13. CLOSING ORDER HISTORY
-------------------------
The popup close button should call:

setOrderHistoryOpen(false);

This changes:

true
 ↓
false

and the popup disappears.


14. DISPLAYING ORDERS
---------------------
The history popup uses:

orderHistory.map((order) => ...)

Because orderHistory is an array, map() allows us to create UI for every order.

Example:

orderHistory = [
  order3,
  order2,
  order1
]

map() creates:

Order 3 UI
Order 2 UI
Order 1 UI


15. IMPORTANT React CONCEPT
---------------------------
The Order History feature demonstrates several important React concepts:

1. useState
   Stores orderHistory and controls the popup.

2. useEffect
   Loads and saves history.

3. localStorage
   Persists history in the browser.

4. JSON.parse
   Converts stored JSON string into JavaScript data.

5. JSON.stringify
   Converts JavaScript data into a string for localStorage.

6. map()
   Displays multiple orders.

7. setState callback
   Safely updates history using the latest state.

8. Conditional rendering
   Displays the popup only when orderHistoryOpen is true.


